+	// The mip level the sprite reads the kernel at. One quarter-resolution pixel spans
+	// 1 / s_q kernel texels - seventeen at f/16, a hundred wide open - and the rays inside the
+	// kernel are one or two texels across, so a single tap lands on a ray about one time in
+	// seventeen and the pattern vanishes from the picture. Reading level log2(1 / s_q) instead
+	// gives the pixel the MEAN intensity over its own footprint in one tap, and the 1 / s_q^2
+	// above then turns that mean into the true integral over the footprint. The shader redoes
+	// this per wavelength (the pattern is stretched by lambda0 / lambda); what is passed is the
+	// ceiling, the coarsest level that exists.
+	const float fMaxLod = (float)max(kernel.GetKernelMipCount() - 1, 0);
+	const float fLodRef = clamp_tpl(logf(max(1.0f / fPitchQuarterPx, 1.0f)) / logf(2.0f), 0.0f, fMaxLod);
+
 	// Spectral sampling. Each wavelength is a SCALED copy of the same pattern -
 	// PSF_lambda(u) = (l0/l)^2 * PSF_l0(u * l0/l) - so blue sits compact near the core and red
 	// runs out to the tips. That is what makes a real sunstar's rays rainbow: dispersion, not a
